meta {
  name: 10 - Issues Bare
  type: graphql
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query IssuesBare {
    issues(first: 2, includeArchived: true, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
      nodes {
        id
        createdAt
        updatedAt
        archivedAt
        number
        identifier
        title
        description
        priority
        priorityLabel
        estimate
        sortOrder
        prioritySortOrder
        subIssueSortOrder
        startedAt
        startedTriageAt
        triagedAt
        completedAt
        canceledAt
        autoClosedAt
        autoArchivedAt
        addedToProjectAt
        addedToCycleAt
        addedToTeamAt
        snoozedUntilAt
        dueDate
        slaStartedAt
        slaBreachesAt
        slaType
        customerTicketCount
        branchName
        url
        trashed
        team { id }
        state { id }
        assignee { id }
        creator { id }
        delegate { id }
        project { id }
        projectMilestone { id }
        cycle { id }
        parent { id }
        snoozedBy { id }
        lastAppliedTemplate { id }
      }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  The canonical Issues selection set — this is verbatim what the connector will send. It
  is the SHAPE reference for the stream: one full representative item, so the document
  model and the discovered schema can be diffed against a real response.

  Design of the selection set (GraphQL returns nothing you do not ask for, so unlike a
  REST connector this list is exhaustive by construction):

  - **Scalars are taken wholesale.** At 0.1 points each they are nearly free, and Estuary's
    schema inference picks up everything the Pydantic model does not declare.
  - **Relations are `{ id }` only — never nested objects.** An object costs 1 point vs a
    scalar's 0.1, so ten `{ id }` relations cost as much as 100 scalars. Expanding even one
    into its own field set would multiply that across all 250 nodes.
  - **No nested CONNECTIONS.** `labels`, `comments`, `history`, `attachments`, `children`,
    `subscribers` and friends are all deliberately omitted. A connection multiplies its
    children's cost by its page size, so a single `labels(first: 20) { nodes { id } }`
    adds ~22 points PER NODE — pushing a 250-row page from ~3,700 to ~9,500 points, right
    against the 10,000 cap. Issue-to-label membership belongs in a separate child stream,
    not in this selection set.

  Static cost arithmetic under the documented formula (30 scalars, 11 relation objects):
  per node = 1 + 30 x 0.1 + 11 x 1.1 = ~16.1 points; at `first: 250` that is ~4,025 points,
  comfortably inside the 10,000-point per-query cap with ~2.5x headroom.

  `includeArchived: true` is set here and on every cluster request — see
  `14 - Issues Include Archived` for the justification.

  **Limitation:** this workspace holds only 4 issues (Linear's stock onboarding set), all
  with `archivedAt: null` and all sharing one `updatedAt`. The saved example proves the
  FIELD SHAPE and the pageInfo contract; it cannot demonstrate archived rows, cursor
  movement, or realistic complexity. See `Seeding/` for the handoff that would close those
  gaps, and `05 - Complexity Envelope` for why the recorded `X-Complexity` does not
  extrapolate.
}

example {
  name: 200 — full representative Issue (2 of 4)

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query IssuesBare {
        issues(first: 2, includeArchived: true, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
          nodes {
            id
            createdAt
            updatedAt
            archivedAt
            number
            identifier
            title
            description
            priority
            priorityLabel
            estimate
            sortOrder
            prioritySortOrder
            subIssueSortOrder
            startedAt
